op7t-0198: show the whole iPhone X picture, shrunk to the panel, instead of cropping it

2026-11-12, user: the keyboard's Q and P keys go off the screen. The UI is
the iPhone X's 1125x2436 (0061); since 0108 the panel scans it out directly
and crops the centred 1080x2400: 22 columns left, 23 right, 18 rows top and
bottom. iOS leaves ~9 px between Q/P and the edge, so half of them is gone.

Each of the two VIG pipes that feed the panel (VIG0 left half, VIG1 right
half) has a QSEED3 scaler (live DT: qcom,sde-qseed-type "qseedv3", at pipe +
0xa00). On a flip of a full-screen surface they now read 563 columns each
(x 0 and 562) and shrink them, bilinear, to 540x2339, placed 30 rows down:
the whole picture at 0.96 with black bars of 30/31 rows (an iPhone 12 mini
shows the same 1125x2436 on its 1080x2340 panel). Register offsets from
Linux sde_hw_sspp.c / sde_hw_util.c; filter 3 = bilinear (sde_drm.h), no
coefficient tables. ABL's pipe values are saved at start and put back for
the splash buffer (copied frames, the power-off fade), which stays 1:1.

Touch follows (SweetHID.cpp): while sweet_fb_scaled is 1, a panel pixel maps
to x / 1080, (y - 30) / 2339, and the edge zone moves with the picture.

sweetscale=0: crop as before. Not used with QSEED3 version 0x1002 (other
phase layout) or if the version reads 0 / all ones. Logged at boot: "HOTDOGB
SCAN: ABL VIG0 ..., qseed version ..." and "scaling on/off".
Not compiled locally (no compiler for the kernel here): the CI build is the
check.

Apply after 0197.
